# Migrating `EventProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Issues with the Original Code

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — removed in .NET 5+
2. **`Thread.Interrupt()` is obsolete** in .NET 8 (still works but deprecated)
3. **Race condition**: multiple enqueues with one `WaitOne`/`Reset` can lose items
4. **No graceful shutdown** mechanism

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private Task? _processTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _processTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken token)
    {
        await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(token))
        {
            Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        if (!_channel.Writer.TryWrite(item))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Failed to enqueue item.");
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete(); // signals no more items
        _cts?.Cancel();            // cancel if stuck waiting for items

        var waitTime = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (_processTask is not null)
            await _processTask.WaitAsync(waitTime);
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item)
    {
        // Your processing logic here
    }
}
```

## If You Need a Synchronous API (Closer to Original)

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();
    private Thread? _thread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop) { IsBackground = true };
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        try
        {
            foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(_cts!.Token))
                Handle(item);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) { /* expected on shutdown */ }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item) => _queue.Add(item);

    public void Stop()
    {
        _queue.CompleteAdding();       // signal end of stream
        _cts?.Cancel();                // unblock if waiting
        if (!_thread?.Join(5000) ?? true)
        {
            // Thread didn't exit — no Abort available, log a warning
            // Consider Environment.FailFast() only for critical scenarios
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _queue.Dispose();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { }
}
```

## Migration Pitfalls Summary

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 Replacement | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationToken` | Abort throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `CancellationToken` | Marked `[Obsolete]` in .NET 8 |
| `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue` | `Channel<T>` or `BlockingCollection<T>` | Eliminates race conditions |
| Manual `lock` + signal | Built-in thread-safe collections | Less error-prone |
| `while(true)` with no exit | Cooperative cancellation via token | Enables graceful shutdown |

## Recommendation

**Prefer the `Channel<T>` approach** (first example) — it's the idiomatic .NET 8 pattern for producer/consumer workloads: allocation-efficient, backpressure-capable, and naturally async.
